+/// Where the picture sits on the canvas: `base` sized, centred, shrinking to a
+/// line for the screen-off animation.
+///
+/// `base` is the picture's size in canvas units, which is *not* the canvas: on
+/// this port the canvas is the 752x560 panel while the picture's natural size
+/// is `GAME_W` x `GAME_H`, a pixel-exact `SCALE` times the source texture, and
+/// the difference is paper. Upstream never had to tell them apart -- its canvas
+/// and its picture were both 720x480, which is why "stretch" filled the screen
+/// there by construction and filled all but a 16px border here until this was
+/// a parameter.
+///
+/// A base larger than the canvas is fine and is how the picture is scaled past
+/// pixel-exact: the draw clamps to the canvas, so the paper is clipped first
+/// and the picture itself is clipped only once it runs out of panel. See
+/// `video_mode::base_size`.
